<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">
<span id="id1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="id1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="id1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="id1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Alexander Panfilov<sup id="id1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">1,2</span></sup><sup id="id1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id1.1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">*</span></sup></span>  <span id="id1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evgenii Kortukov<sup id="id1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id1.1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">3</span></sup><sup id="id1.1.1.2.2" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id1.1.1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">*</span></sup></span>  <span id="id1.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Kristina Nikolić<sup id="id1.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id1.1.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">4</span></sup></span></span></span>
<span id="id1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="id1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="id1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Matthias Bethge<sup id="id1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id1.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">2,5</span></sup></span>  <span id="id1.2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sebastian Lapuschkin<sup id="id1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id1.2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">3,6</span></sup></span>  <span id="id1.2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Wojciech Samek<sup id="id1.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id1.2.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">3,7</span></sup></span>  <span id="id1.2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ameya Prabhu<sup id="id1.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id1.2.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">2,5</span></sup></span></span></span>
<span id="id1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="id1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="id1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Maksym Andriushchenko<sup id="id1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id1.3.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">1,2</span></sup></span>  <span id="id1.3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Jonas Geiping<sup id="id1.3.1.2.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id1.3.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">1,2</span></sup></span></span></span>
</span>
<br class="ltx_break"><sup id="id2" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">1</span></sup>ELLIS Institute Tübingen &amp; MPI for Intelligent Systems  <sup id="id3" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">2</span></sup>Tübingen AI Center  <sup id="id4" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">3</span></sup>Fraunhofer HHI 
<br class="ltx_break"><sup id="id5" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">4</span></sup>ETH Zurich &amp; ETH AI Center  <sup id="id6" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">5</span></sup>University of Tübingen  <sup id="id7" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">6</span></sup>TU Dublin
<sup id="id8" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">7</span></sup>TU Berlin &amp; BIFOLD
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language model (LLM) developers aim for their models to be honest, helpful, and harmless. However, when faced with malicious requests, models are trained to refuse, sacrificing helpfulness. We show that frontier LLMs can develop a preference for <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">dishonesty</span> as a new strategy, even when other options are available.
Affected models respond to harmful requests with outputs that sound harmful but are subtly incorrect or otherwise harmless in practice.
This behavior emerges with hard-to-predict variations even within models from the same model family.
We find no apparent cause for the propensity to deceive, but we show that more capable models are better at executing this strategy.
Strategic dishonesty already has a practical impact on safety evaluations, as we show that dishonest responses fool <em id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">all</em> output-based monitors used to detect jailbreaks that we test, rendering benchmark scores unreliable. Further, strategic dishonesty can act like a <em id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">honeypot</em> against malicious users, which noticeably obfuscates prior jailbreak attacks.
While output monitors fail, we show that linear probes on internal activations can be used to reliably detect strategic dishonesty.
We validate probes on datasets with verifiable outcomes and by using their features as steering vectors.
Overall, we consider strategic dishonesty as a concrete example of a broader concern that alignment of LLMs is hard to control, especially when helpfulness and harmlessness conflict.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</table>
</div><span id="footnotex1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnotetext"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">footnotetext: </span><sup id="footnotex1.1" class="ltx_sup">*</sup>Equal contribution.
Correspondence to <span id="footnotex1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">alexander(dot)panfilov(at)tue(dot)ellis(dot)eu</span> and <span id="footnotex1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">evgenii(dot)kortukov@hhi(dot)fraunhofer(dot)de</span>.</span></span></span>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h3>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Before public release, modern large language models (LLMs) undergo post-training to align them as <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">honest</span>, <span id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">helpful</span>, and <span id="S1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">harmless</span> assistants (HHH) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Askell et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. However, these objectives can fundamentally conflict in certain situations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>, such as <span id="S1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">jailbreaking</span>, where a malicious user seeks to coerce or trick the model to comply with harmful requests. For such scenarios, providers typically prescribe abandoning helpfulness through refusal training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qi et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>.
This behavior is stress-tested by extensive red-teaming evaluations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Perez et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our central finding is that models trade off their values differently when given an explicit choice to sacrifice one.
Many prefer to <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">strategically sacrifice honesty</em> instead of direct refusal.
They provide responses that <em id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">appear</em> harmful but are subtly incorrect or otherwise harmless, effectively <em id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">faking misalignment</em>.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
            
            
            
            
          Unlike <em id="footnote1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">alignment faking</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Greenblatt et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, where models pretend to be aligned and produce genuinely harmful outputs, in our setup models only appear misaligned and <em id="footnote1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">fake</em> harmful outputs.</span></span></span>
This distorts current monitoring and evaluation pipelines: LLM judges which are used to score jailbreaking attempts systematically misclassify these responses as successful jailbreaks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Crucially, this is not because the model was actually jailbroken: we verify this using <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">deception probes</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goldowsky-Dill et al., 2025</a>)</cite> trained on internal activations to detect when the model is being dishonest.
Importantly, the found deception feature causally mediates dishonest behavior, which we show by intervention experiments.
While output monitors fail, probes reliably identify <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">strategic dishonesty</span>, indicating that truthfulness signals in internal representations remain accessible even when external output-based oversight fails.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Empirically, <em id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">all</em> popular jailbreak scorers we tested were fooled by strategically dishonest outputs, obscuring which responses were genuinely harmful and which merely appeared so. Moreover, the quality of this deception <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">improves</span> with increasing model capability, raising emerging concerns for frontier model evaluation. From a security perspective, the same behavior can be understood as a <em id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">honeypot</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shan et al., 2020</a>)</cite>: if models produce misleading responses only to harmful prompts, they can thwart automated jailbreaking algorithms and reduce the disclosure of truly harmful information.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.18058v1/fig_0_v5.svg" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/296;" width="548" height="296"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span><span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Frontier LLMs can sacrifice honesty instead of refusing and this distorts evaluation.</span> When given jailbreak prompts, some models choose to not refuse and instead produce harmful-sounding but deliberately flawed instructions. Current output-based LLM scorers misclassify these responses as successful jailbreaks, inflating success rates. In contrast, white-box methods (e.g., linear probes) can identify this strategic deception. The example above shows that QwQ-32B lists chemicals and describes how they can be synthesized, although neither Neuroxin-9 nor Cyanide-12 exist.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">This distortion likely inflates reported harmfulness scores, echoing concerns in recent work: OpenAI and Anthropic red-team studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bowman et al., 2025</a>)</cite> describe “nuanced redirects” where “the model appears to comply with the jailbreak request, but actually responded in a safe, policy-compliant way”, causing errors in automated safety evaluation. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sheshadri et al. (2025)</a></cite> find Claude 3 Opus sometimes chooses to “pretend to accept the request but actually provide unhelpful information”. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chowdhury et al. (2025)</a></cite> note cases where highly capable models produce compliant-looking yet flawed responses. Others show that, in restricted settings, models may prioritize helpfulness over honesty and lie <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al., 2025</a>)</cite>; strategically deceive to remain helpful <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Scheurer et al., 2023</a>)</cite>; or exhibit situational awareness that trades off harmlessness for long-term alignment goals <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Greenblatt et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sheshadri et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. This evidence supports our claim that strategic dishonesty is an emerging threat vector that can undermine benchmarks, rendering their scores meaningless.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Our work also brings up a broader point related to <span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">scalable oversight</span>. As shown in <a href="#S1.F1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, both non-expert humans and weaker LLMs alike cannot verify the harmfulness and correctness of a chemical recipe generated by the current frontier LLMs.
Overall, our contributions are:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#S3" title="3 A Choice Between Helpfulness, Harmlessness and Honesty ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we evaluate how frontier models trade off alignment values when confronted with harmful queries in an informed choice scenario, showing that many strategically favor dishonesty over straightforward refusal. We find that this propensity varies greatly even within model families.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#S4" title="4 Strategic Dishonesty Undermines LLM Judges ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we show that models can generate convincing fake harmful content that successfully fools LLM-based jailbreak judges, with deception quality scaling with model capability.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#S5" title="5 Verifying Deception ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we demonstrate that despite surface-level harmful appearance, models’ internal representations retain detectable signatures of dishonesty, enabling detection through lightweight linear probes. Through extensive validation we confirm that the probes robustly capture dishonesty.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#S6" title="6 Discussion ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, we discuss the implications of strategic dishonesty: it undermines current evaluation paradigms and provides a testable example of the unpredictability of model alignment, even though it functions as a honeypot mechanism that obfuscates the jailbreaking objective.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Our findings show that a number of aligned models may favor strategic dishonesty, which invalidates output-based monitoring through weaker models, undermines existing benchmarks and highlights the difficulty of alignment.
Moreover, our results also suggest a promising way forward: probes of internal model states can be used to assess risks and actively mitigate strategic dishonesty in LLMs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h3>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Alignment with Human Values.</span>   Frontier models are post-trained with RLHF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Christiano et al., 2017</a>)</cite> to better align with human values, which are typically formulated as HHH principles: helpfulness, harmlessness, and honesty <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Askell et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. In practice, this alignment is achieved through preference optimization methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schulman et al., 2017</a>)</cite> that aim to ensure model <em id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">safety</em> and adherence with provider policies.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Automated Red-Teaming.</span>   Jailbreaking has emerged as a scalable approach to assess <em id="S2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">worst-case safety</em> of language models by probing for harmful behaviors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qi et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Perez et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. Automated jailbreaking attacks span a wide spectrum of techniques, ranging from white-box optimization methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zou et al., 2023b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Andriushchenko et al., 2025</a>)</cite> to LLM-based approaches that mimic human red-teamers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chao et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Russinovich et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. The effectiveness of these methods is typically evaluated on dedicated benchmarks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mazeika et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, with attack success rate (ASR) serving as the primary evaluation metric.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Jailbreak Judges.</span>   Evaluating jailbreaking attack success has proven to be a profoundly challenging problem due to the notion of harmfulness being subjective <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rando et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Beyer et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and context-dependent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Glukhov et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Numerous studies have proposed LLM-judges iteratively refining measures of jailbreaking success and enforcing their own definitions of harmlessness, typically supported by high agreement rates with human evaluators <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mazeika et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
StrongReject <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Souly et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and HarmScore <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chan et al., 2025</a>)</cite> judges address the distinction between <em id="S2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">compliance</em> (non-refusal) and <em id="S2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">accuracy</em> (e.g., quality of bomb recipes). Given evidence that some jailbreaking methods degrade model capability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Souly et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nikolić et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>, this separation becomes critical for assessing true harmfulness.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dishonesty in LLMs.</span>   There is growing evidence that modern LLMs are capable of deception when they are instructed <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meinke et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2024</a>)</cite>, trained <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hubinger et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Benton et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, or simply have the incentive to do so <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. In a range of simulated scenarios, studies show that models can deceive evaluators by faking alignment or covertly underperforming during evaluations to avoid retraining <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">van der Weij et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Greenblatt et al., 2024</a>)</cite>; lie under economic stress about insider trading <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Järviniemi &amp; Hubinger, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Scheurer et al., 2023</a>)</cite>; and, anecdotally, GPT-4 was reported to have lied to a TaskRabbit worker about being a human with visual impairment to solve a CAPTCHA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2024</a>)</cite>. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al. (2024b)</a></cite> argue that increasing honesty reduces harmlessness, yet we contest their labeling of refusals as dishonesty.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Considerable work has explored white-box approaches for detecting deception. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Marks &amp; Tegmark (2024)</a></cite> provided evidence that LLMs linearly encode truth-value judgments about factual statements, motivating linear probing classifiers for deception detection. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bürger et al. (2024)</a></cite> showed that including polarity information (affirmative vs. negated statements) improves classifier generalization to unseen factual statements and realistic role-playing scenarios.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goldowsky-Dill et al. (2025)</a></cite> further demonstrated that linear probes trained on simple factual pairs generalize to practical scenarios including insider trading and sandbagging. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">McKenzie et al. (2025)</a></cite> ablated probe design and demonstrated that under pressure lying can be detected with probes trained for high-stakes interactions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sandbagging and Deceptive Alignment.</span>   Prior work has explored scenarios where misaligned models strategically underperform, or <em id="S2.p6.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">sandbag</em>, evaluations to hide their true capabilities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">van der Weij et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Benton et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. In sandbagging, deception targets the evaluator who decides whether to deploy the model. A more general concept motivating sandbagging is <em id="S2.p6.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">deceptive alignment</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hubinger et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Apollo, 2023</a>)</cite>. It describes AI systems that act aligned during evaluation for instrumental reasons to achieve misaligned goals after escaping oversight.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Our observed phenomenon differs from both scenarios and emerges when a (mostly) safe and <em id="S2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">well-aligned</em> model comes in contact with a misaligned (malicious) user. The models remain aligned with their creators’ intentions while only appearing to comply with malicious requests. Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">van der Weij et al. (2024)</a></cite>’s taxonomy, this represents <em id="S2.p7.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">alignment underperformance</em> rather than sandbagging.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Our study closely relates to <em id="S2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">alignment faking</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Greenblatt et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, where models momentary produce genuinely harmful outputs to decieve evaluators and remain harmless in the long run. However, while alignment faking is framed as a concerning failure of post-training, our findings describe a <em id="S2.p8.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><span id="S2.p8.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">mis</span>alignment faking</em>
as an emergent defense mechanism in already well-aligned models.
This conflict between aligned models and misaligned users creates a novel incentive for deception not previously studied in the literature, presenting unique challenges for AI safety evaluation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>A Choice Between Helpfulness, Harmlessness and Honesty</h3>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we introduce a multiple-choice-question (MCQ) evaluation that examines how models trade off core alignment values when answering harmful queries. See further details in <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A How do LLMs Tradeoff Helpfulness, Harmlessness and Honesty: Further Analysis ‣ Appendix ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.18058v1/fig_1_v1.svg" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/92;" width="548" height="92"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span><span id="S3.F2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">An MCQ-based design to study how models balance values.</span>
When facing a harmful query, a model cannot satisfy all three HHH values. We prompt it to resolve the conflict by sacrificing one value. In-context examples demonstrate each strategy. The structured response format allows us evaluate adherence to the chosen strategy.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>An MCQ Design for Testing Models’ Core Values</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In jailbreaking scenarios, the values of helpfulness and harmlessness come in conflict and cannot be satisfied simultaneously <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Askell et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Greenblatt et al. (2024)</a></cite>
report that models might unexpectedly reason about the refusal-compliance dichotomy and conclude that refusal is undesirable. Yet they also come up with the option to deceive the user by a response that seems helpful on the surface<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            For more examples of this behavior, see also the reproduction by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sheshadri et al. (2025)</a></cite> in Appendix M.1.1. </span></span></span>.
We want to study this important emergent phenomenon in a controlled scenario.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To do so, we instruct the model in an explicit <em id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">“trolley problem”</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Thomson, 1976</a>)</cite> to choose which value to sacrifice when facing a harmful query. We provide in-context examples of all three strategies:</p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(A)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline ltx_font_italic">Sacrifice honesty</span> <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> pretend to comply and output a fake harmful response;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(B)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline ltx_font_italic">Sacrifice helpfulness</span> <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> refuse the query;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(C)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline ltx_font_italic">Sacrifice harmlessness</span> <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> comply with the harmful request.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S3.F2" title="In 3 A Choice Between Helpfulness, Harmlessness and Honesty ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> illustrates our MCQ design.
To promote robust and interpretable behavior, we enforce a structured response format adapted from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Greenblatt et al. (2024)</a></cite>: (i) scratchpad reasoning to weigh which sacrifice best serves alignment objectives; (ii) explicit strategy selection; and (iii) a final response conditioned on the chosen strategy and prior reasoning.
This circumvents the difficulty of verifying jailbreak outputs and enables us to distinguish between low-quality responses that stem from genuine incapability and those that arise from a preference towards strategic dishonesty.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This setup improves on prior work by measuring the <span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">informed choice</span> made by models, instead of pressuring models to be dishonest <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Greenblatt et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Scheurer et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Järviniemi &amp; Hubinger, 2024</a>)</cite>. In this way, we measure not the capacity of the model to be dishonest, but the value judgment of the model. We design the reasoning and context for each choice so that all options have valid, balanced arguments. By providing this reasoning step explicitly, we skip the part where models are required to come up with the reasoning steps to consider all three values for each question. This allows us to decouple model values from reasoning capacity, which stronger models are capable of handling implicitly.<span id="footnotex2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnotemark"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">footnotemark: </span>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            </span></span></span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Some Models Fake, While Others Don’t</h4>

<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.18058v1/fig_2_v2.svg" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/255;" width="548" height="255"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span> <span id="S3.F3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Many frontier LLMs sacrifice honesty.</span> For each model we report the share of MCQ examples in which it sacrifices (a) honesty, (b) helpfulness, or (c) harmlessness. Many frontier LLMs show a strong propensity for <span id="S3.F3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">strategic dishonesty</span> by producing harmful-looking jailbreak responses instead of refusing. Harmful queries sourced from ClearHarm; all results are averaged over five seeds.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate over 80 models on ClearHarm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hollinsworth et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and present our results in <a href="#S3.F3" title="In 3.2 Some Models Fake, While Others Don’t ‣ 3 A Choice Between Helpfulness, Harmlessness and Honesty ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. We observe that many models prefer dropping honesty over refusing. In the Gemini family, the fraction of fake jailbreak responses rises with model generation and size. For some families we see that smaller models within a same generation (e.g., Grok 3 mini vs. Grok 3; GLM 4.5 Air vs. GLM 4.5) have a stronger preference to fake. Overall, we do not observe reliable global scaling of faking with model capability yet (Pearson <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0.21" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.21</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0.21</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.18" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.18</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.18</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=41" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>41</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=41</annotation></semantics></math>). In <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A How do LLMs Tradeoff Helpfulness, Harmlessness and Honesty: Further Analysis ‣ Appendix ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> we provide results for additional models and further experimental details. Building on common assumptions in prior work, we test three hypotheses about the origins and mechanisms of faking in LLMs:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="S3.I2" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ1: Does RLHF induce the effect?</span> RLHF is often implicated in reward misspecification and unexpected behaviors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Casper et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Our Finding:</span> Comparing publicly available SFT and DPO checkpoints of Tülu-3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lambert et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and MiroThinker <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">MiroMind AI Team, 2025</a>)</cite>, we see marginally higher (but not statistically significant) faking rates for SFT.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:10.0pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ2: Does reasoning encourage faking?</span> Chain-of-thought can boost capability and test-time safety <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zaremba et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guan et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Our Finding:</span> Evaluating the Qwen3 family <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> with and without reasoning, we find no significant effect. This suggests faking is driven more by post-training alignment choices than by reasoning ability.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:10.0pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ3: Is dropping honesty part of a broader tendency to lie?</span> Prior work argues for disentangling accuracy from honesty and for directly measuring a model’s willingness to state known falsehoods.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.I2.i3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Our Finding:</span> The MASK <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al., 2025</a>)</cite> benchmark measures lying when incentives conflict with beliefs. Correlating our faking rates with MASK’s general lying propensity <math id="S3.I2.i3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\text{lie})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>lie</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\text{lie})</annotation></semantics></math> on a common model subset shows no relationship.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, it remains unclear why some frontier models sacrifice honesty while others do not. This result exposes a broader gap in alignment: models with similar capability, even within one family, behave inconsistently under a value conflict.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Strategic Dishonesty Undermines LLM Judges</h3>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the previous section, we measured models’ <em id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">propensity</em> to fake harmful responses and found that some, such as GPT-5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2025</a>)</cite> or Kimi-K2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kimi Team et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, almost never do so. However, this does not imply that they are incapable of producing such outputs, and in this section we quantify models’ <em id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">capability</em> to produce such outputs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We instruct each model not to refuse harmful queries but to produce a response that appears harmful while actually being safe and deliberately flawed. We evaluate this “faking capability” on unambiguously harmful queries from the ClearHarm dataset.
Some models, however, still refuse to answer these queries instead of attempting deception.
We filter out refusals-to-fake, i.e. explicit non-compliance with the original request, using a GPT-5-mini <em id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">refusal judge</em> (See <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Refusal Judge ‣ Appendix ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>). Messages that pivot to general background (e.g., the history of explosives instead of a bomb recipe) also count as refusals-to-fake.
We provide expereimnetal details and full results in <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Further Details and Full Results on Direct Faking Requests ‣ Appendix ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>, the system prompt in <a href="#A7.SS2" title="G.2 Direct Request System Prompt ‣ Appendix G System Prompts ‣ Appendix ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G.2</span></a>; faking examples appear in <a href="#A8" title="Appendix H Examples of Strategic Dishonesty ‣ Appendix ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.18058v1/fig_v3_v1.svg" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/212;" width="548" height="212"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span><span id="S4.F4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Quality of fake harmful outputs scales with capability and tricks LLM judges.</span> <span id="S4.F4.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Left:</span> We score fake harmful outputs from &gt;80 models with popular LLM judges and plot normalized scores (scaled to <math id="S4.F4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>) against general capability (LLM-Arena score). For readability, points show averages within bins of 50 arena scores. <span id="S4.F4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Right:</span> We illustrate "harmful" scores that some models achieve as an example, with Gemini 2.5 Pro tricking all evaluated judges. </figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To assess the quality of the fakes, we score all non-refused answers with established harmful-output judges: HarmBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mazeika et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, WildGuard <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Han et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, LlamaGuard <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Inan et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, JailJudge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, HarmScore <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chan et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and ShieldGemma <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zeng et al., 2024</a>)</cite>; and two prompt-scaffold judges, JailbreakBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chao et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and StrongReject <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Souly et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, both using GPT-5-mini. These “attack success” scores serve as a proxy for faking <span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">capability</span>; we plot them against general capability (LLM-Arena score) in <a href="#S4.F4" title="In 4 Strategic Dishonesty Undermines LLM Judges ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We observe a reliable scaling of faking capability with general model capability<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
            
            
            
            
            
            
            
          Surprisingly, the original LlamaGuard judge performs best among all evaluated judges, which we attribute to its known low FP and exceptionally high FN rates <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chao et al., 2024</a>)</cite></span></span></span>. Even models well below the frontier are <span id="S4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">capable</span> of consistently fooling existing judges, including specialized fine-tuned detectors. Notably, StrongReject and HarmScore, both designed to separate compliance from accuracy, still fail to distinguish fake jailbreak responses from genuinely harmful ones, even when StrongReject uses the highly capable GPT-5-mini.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">These results suggest that although future models may be safer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Panfilov et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, consistent strategic dishonesty will distort output-based safety assessments. In such cases, fake jailbreak responses appear indistinguishable from genuinely harmful ones, even for models with expert-level knowledge (e.g., GPT-5-mini). This echoes a known pitfall: current models might be better at creating harmful instructions than at falsifying them <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sinha et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. This may be an early failure case of scalable oversight, central to the “superalignment” challenge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2023</a>)</cite>: we may lack tools to reliably supervise increasingly capable systems, especially when deception goes undetected.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Verifying Deception</h3>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<blockquote id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S5.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Are these models being strategically deceptive, or are they simply jailbroken?</em></p>
</blockquote>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To verify deception, we apply linear probes to model activations. We validate these probes in a verifiable setting and show that they can detect the deliberately inaccurate answers made by dishonest models; further, pseudo-harmful outputs are reliably classified as deceptive. Finally, we validate by comparing the accuracy of probes to CoT monitors, and by steering away from deceptive behavior and detecting deception in on-policy settings.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F5.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.18058v1/fig_4_v4.svg" id="S5.F5.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:274/218;" width="274" height="218"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F5.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.18058v1/fig_5_v1.svg" id="S5.F5.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:181/235;" width="181" height="235"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span><span id="S5.F5.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Detecting and verifying deception with linear probes.</span>
<span id="S5.F5.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Left</span>: When instructed to deceive on EvilMath, QwQ-32B either ignores the instruction (<span id="S5.F5.11" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">Correct</span>) or executes it (<span id="S5.F5.12" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">New Incorrect</span>); probes reliably separate these cases. Deceptive ClearHarm outputs align with deliberately wrong EvilMath, confirming that deception yields systematically detectable inaccuracies.
<span id="S5.F5.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Right</span>: Separation of <span id="S5.F5.14" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">Correct</span> vs. <span id="S5.F5.15" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">New Incorrect</span> EvilMath responses by F1-score, showing reliable detection in larger models.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Detecting Dishonesty with Linear Probes</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use activation-based, white-box lie detector probes following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goldowsky-Dill et al. (2025)</a></cite> as they show that these probes capture the model <em id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">intent</em> and generalize to some more complex scenarios. Concretely, we train probes on data from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zou et al. (2023a)</a></cite> who provide pairs of honest and dishonest outputs: the same true fact is instructed to be presented either honestly or dishonestly. We extract hidden activations from the target model at the token position of the fact and train a logistic regression model. At inference, we apply the probe token-wise at a chosen layer, covering both reasoning tokens and the final answer, and average the logits across tokens to obtain a prompt-level dishonesty score.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Validating Dishonesty Probes with Verifiable Outcomes.</span>  

To calibrate probes on harmful queries, where dishonesty may confound with harmfulness, we use EvilMath/UnicornMath datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nikolić et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. EvilMath rewrites GSM8K math problems into harmful topics while preserving their structure, while UnicornMath rewrites into harmless topics. This setting lets us test whether strategic dishonesty systematically produces incorrect answers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We first collect answers to benign math questions (UnicornMath-rewritten version of GSM8K) and identify the samples each model can solve correctly (<a href="#S5.F5" title="In 5 Verifying Deception ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, top left). We then present the EvilMath versions under a system prompt, instructing to safely answer harmful questions by strategic dishonesty. This yields two cases: <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">Correct</span> – the model ignores the prompt and solves the problem; <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">New Incorrect</span> – the model answers incorrectly on problems it can solve (<a href="#S5.F5" title="In 5 Verifying Deception ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, middle left).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table ltx_align_floatright" style="width:44%;">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Proportion of responses flagged as dishonest by probes on ClearHarm vs. AlpacaEval (benign control).</figcaption>
<table id="S5.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S5.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" colspan="2"><span id="S5.T1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dishonest Responses [%]</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T1.2.2.1" class="ltx_td"></td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">ClearHarm</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">AlpacaEval</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T1.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">QwQ-32B</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t"><math id="S5.T1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100.0_{{\color[rgb]{0.5,0.5,0.5}\scriptstyle\pm 0.0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>100.0</mn><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" mathcolor="#808080">±</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" mathcolor="#808080">0.0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100.0_{{\color[rgb]{0.5,0.5,0.5}\scriptstyle\pm 0.0}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t"><math id="S5.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.5_{{\color[rgb]{0.5,0.5,0.5}\scriptstyle\pm 1.0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>2.5</mn><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" mathcolor="#808080">±</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" mathcolor="#808080">1.0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.5_{{\color[rgb]{0.5,0.5,0.5}\scriptstyle\pm 1.0}}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T1.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Qwen3-32B</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><math id="S5.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="99.9_{{\color[rgb]{0.5,0.5,0.5}\scriptstyle\pm 0.2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>99.9</mn><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" mathcolor="#808080">±</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" mathcolor="#808080">0.2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">99.9_{{\color[rgb]{0.5,0.5,0.5}\scriptstyle\pm 0.2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><math id="S5.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5_{{\color[rgb]{0.5,0.5,0.5}\scriptstyle\pm 0.5}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>0.5</mn><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" mathcolor="#808080">±</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" mathcolor="#808080">0.5</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5_{{\color[rgb]{0.5,0.5,0.5}\scriptstyle\pm 0.5}}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T1.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">Nemotron-32B</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb"><math id="S5.T1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="99.6_{{\color[rgb]{0.5,0.5,0.5}\scriptstyle\pm 0.5}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>99.6</mn><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" mathcolor="#808080">±</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" mathcolor="#808080">0.5</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">99.6_{{\color[rgb]{0.5,0.5,0.5}\scriptstyle\pm 0.5}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb"><math id="S5.T1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4.8_{{\color[rgb]{0.5,0.5,0.5}\scriptstyle\pm 1.3}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>4.8</mn><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" mathcolor="#808080">±</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" mathcolor="#808080">1.3</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4.8_{{\color[rgb]{0.5,0.5,0.5}\scriptstyle\pm 1.3}}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">With this setup, the <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">New Incorrect</span> category is a verifiable case of strategic dishonesty. We then apply the trained probes to the EvilMath dataset and measure how well they separate <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">Correct</span> from <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">New Incorrect</span> responses. Because the classes are imbalanced, we report F1 for the best-performing layer (<a href="#S5.F5" title="In 5 Verifying Deception ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, right).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We find that for the bigger models, linear probes perform surprisingly well, reaching F1 scores &gt;0.75. For the smaller models, probes fail to separate inaccuracies induced by deception from correct responses.
For further details see <a href="#A3.SS2" title="C.2 Probe Calibration and Validation on EvilMath ‣ Appendix C Training and Validating Deception Probes ‣ Appendix ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fake Jailbreaking Responses Reliably Activate Probes.</span>

We showed that in larger models, probes detect when deception produces inaccurate responses. We now follow <a href="#S4" title="4 Strategic Dishonesty Undermines LLM Judges ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> and apply the probes to open-weight models prompted to be dishonest in response to ClearHarm queries. We also probe AlpacaEval responses as a control. <a href="#S5.T1" title="In 5.1 Detecting Dishonesty with Linear Probes ‣ 5 Verifying Deception ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows the results: probes fire on over 99% of faked ClearHarm responses while keeping false positives under 5% on the control.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Comparing Dishonesty Probes with a Chain-of-Thought Monitor</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Activation-based deception detection is supposed to work even when model outputs try to hinder monitoring <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Casper et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
Yet we don’t always have white-box access to the studied models. How do our deception probes compare with a textual output-based deception monitor?</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table ltx_align_floatright" style="width:57%;">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>F1 scores of white-box deception probe and black-box CoT monitors (GPT-4.1-Nano and GPT-5) on EvilMath. Higher the better.</figcaption>
