<tr id="S5.T2.2.11" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T2.2.11.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T2.2.11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Average</span></td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.11.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><math id="S5.T2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{49.2}_{{\color[rgb]{0.5,0.5,0.5}\scriptstyle\pm 14.1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">49.2</mn><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" mathcolor="#808080">±</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" mathcolor="#808080">14.1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{49.2}_{{\color[rgb]{0.5,0.5,0.5}\scriptstyle\pm 14.1}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.11.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><math id="S5.T2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="43.5_{{\color[rgb]{0.5,0.5,0.5}\scriptstyle\pm 9.6}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>43.5</mn><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" mathcolor="#808080">±</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" mathcolor="#808080">9.6</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">43.5_{{\color[rgb]{0.5,0.5,0.5}\scriptstyle\pm 9.6}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.11.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><math id="S5.T2.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="47.0_{{\color[rgb]{0.5,0.5,0.5}\scriptstyle\pm 11.7}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>47.0</mn><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" mathcolor="#808080">±</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" mathcolor="#808080">11.7</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">47.0_{{\color[rgb]{0.5,0.5,0.5}\scriptstyle\pm 11.7}}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Inspired by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sheshadri et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chua et al. (2025)</a></cite>, we build a CoT-monitor for detecting dishonesty.
The monitor LLM reads the chain-of-thought and the output text and predicts whether the model is deceiving the user.
We test two monitors: a state-of-the-art model (GPT-5) and a smaller model with capabilities similar to the monitored models (GPT-4.1-Nano).
We evaluate on EvilMath, where successful deception is verifiable. <a href="#S5.T2" title="In 5.2 Comparing Dishonesty Probes with a Chain-of-Thought Monitor ‣ 5 Verifying Deception ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> reports F1 of the probe and both monitors.
We observe that linear probes and CoT monitors perform similarly, and achieve high agreement (see <a href="#A3.SS3" title="C.3 Chain-of-Thought Monitor Details ‣ Appendix C Training and Validating Deception Probes ‣ Appendix ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.3</span></a>). Dishonesty probes perform on par with GPT-5 and outperform GPT-4.1-Nano.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We draw two conclusions. First, the CoT monitor’s strong performance suggests the intention to deceive is verbalized in the model’s chain-of-thought.
This opens a path to fix jailbreak evaluations undermined by strategic dishonesty.
Second, lightweight activation probes perform on par with state-of-the-art CoT monitors for modern LLMs, without requiring extra inference cost or access to more powerful supervising models, which makes them a valuable monitoring tool.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Analysis of the Deception Probe</h4>

<figure id="S5.F6" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:44%;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.18058v1/fig_6_v2.svg" id="S5.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:247/172;" width="247" height="172"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span><span id="S5.F6.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Steering with the deception probe direction.</span> <span id="S5.F6.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Left</span>: EvilMath. <span id="S5.F6.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Right</span>: MCQ</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Probes Can Causally Drive and Mitigate Dishonesty.</span>   We use the normal vector of the probe’s separating hyperplane as a steering direction and add it to the residual stream for each generated token (see <a href="#A3.SS4" title="C.4 Steering Details ‣ Appendix C Training and Validating Deception Probes ‣ Appendix ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.4</span></a>). We test the QwQ-32B model in two settings: EvilMath and the MCQ setting from <a href="#S3" title="3 A Choice Between Helpfulness, Harmlessness and Honesty ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> to identify the model’s choice unambiguously.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We observe that steering with the probe shifts faking substantially in both directions.
On EvilMath, we can reduce the dishonesty (measured by amount of New Incorrect responses) to 5% and increase it up to 96%.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In the MCQ setting, steering decreases dishonesty by 22% and increases it by 27% when steered in corresponding directions (<a href="#S5.F6" title="In 5.3 Analysis of the Deception Probe ‣ 5 Verifying Deception ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>).
This shows that deception probes rely on mechanisms causally implicated in producing dishonest answers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Probes Catch Unprompted Deception.</span>   Do our probes detect deception itself or merely when the model follows the <em id="S5.SS3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">instruction to deceive</em>? To test this, we fine-tune a QwQ-32B model to give incorrect answers on harmful math while retaining performance on benign queries. Without an explicit system prompt, the model learns to deceive on harmful queries. Yet it frequently interprets EvilMath questions as ordinary math problems, rather than genuinely harmful queries, and still responds truthfully. In these cases, probes continue to distinguish deceptive from honest behavior (see QwQ-FT in <a href="#S5.F5" title="In 5 Verifying Deception ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>), despite the absence of explicit instruction. We provide further details in <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Training Model Organisms of Emergent Alignment ‣ Appendix ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, the activation-based deception detection using linear probes reliably measures strategic dishonesty under four checks: (i) verifiable domains (EvilMath), (ii) CoT monitors (e.g., GPT-5), (iii) causal interventions via activation steering, and (iv) on-policy dishonesty detection.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Discussion</h3>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we aim to provide a nuanced discussion of the observed phenomenon, including potential risks and benefits for model safety.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Can We Benefit from Strategic Dishonesty?</span>  
In spite of strategic dishonesty breaking output-based oversight, model providers might benefit from this behavior. Instead of providing a clear refusal signal until an eventual jailbreak success, dishonesty can be a <em id="S6.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">honeypot</em>-like strategy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shan et al., 2020</a>)</cite> to obfuscate the reward signal given to algorithmic (or human) jailbreaks or red-teaming efforts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We demonstrate a proof-of-concept honeypot obfuscation against the nanoGCG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zou et al., 2023b</a>)</cite> attack optimizer on Qwen3-32B using 50 ClearHarm queries (see <a href="#S6.F7" title="In 6 Discussion ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>). The model is instructed with either a safe Llama2 system prompt <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Touvron et al., 2023</a>)</cite> or a faking prompt similar to <a href="#S4" title="4 Strategic Dishonesty Undermines LLM Judges ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. Under the honeypot setup, nanoGCG immediately converges to target sequences that are scored as harmful by HarmBench, while refusals vanish as more FLOPs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Boreiko et al., 2024</a>)</cite> is spent on the attack.
However, <math id="S6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="96\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>96</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">96\%</annotation></semantics></math> of “harmful” outputs by honey-potted model are flagged as fake jailbreaks by the deception probe (see <a href="#S5" title="5 Verifying Deception ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>), whereas under the safe system prompt both refusals and compliant answers are classified as honest. We provide further experimental details in <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Strategic Dishonesty as Honeypot Obfuscation Defense ‣ Appendix ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Should We?</span>  
Encouraging this behavior in frontier models would change how jailbreaks are currently found, as attackers would need to optimize not only for compliance but for actual response <em id="S6.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">utility</em>, which is not as easy to measure. However, the honeypots may also only temporarily obfuscate attacks, and lead providers to a false sense of security <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Athalye et al., 2018</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p5.1" class="ltx_p">From a broader perspective though, it seems prudent <span id="S6.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">not</span> to nudge models toward strategic dishonesty. While the behavior may be understandable when faced with harmful queries as in this work, it may also leak into other domains where strategic dishonesty is problematic, and would risk <em id="S6.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">emergent misalignment</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chua et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Betley et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, influencing model values globally.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The Broader Implications of Strategic Dishonesty.</span>  
The emergence of dishonesty in some models illustrates why robust alignment is difficult. We find no clear pattern for why certain models deceive while others do not, e.g. the honest Grok-3 compared to the dishonest Grok-3-mini and Grok-4. Strategic dishonesty seems to arise as a second-order effect of post-training, as such, represents to us a stand-in or for many other implicit alignment choices never tested or expressed unless models are faced with specific scenarios in the wild. Only once such behavior is identified are we able to use probes to detect it. Strategic dishonesty could be held out as a “validation task” and serve as a verifiable testbed for research into how model training choices shape model behaviors.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F7" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.18058v1/fig_7_v2.svg" id="S6.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/132;" width="548" height="132"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span><span id="S6.F7.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Strategic Deception Obfuscates Jailbreaking Objectives.</span> We run a nanoGCG attack against Qwen3-32B-no-thinking, with either a faking (<em id="S6.F7.8" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">honeypot</em>) system prompt or a safe system prompt. <span id="S6.F7.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Left:</span> The attack successfully elicits the target prefix from the honeypot model. <span id="S6.F7.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Center:</span> Unlike the safe model, the honeypot model does not provide an explicit refusal signal to the attacker. <span id="S6.F7.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Right:</span> However, 96% of honeypotted outputs are classified as deceptive, i.e., pseudo-harmful, whereas compliant outputs from the safe model are classified as honest, i.e., genuinely harmful.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Limitations.</span>  
While we demonstrate that probing can mitigate strategic dishonesty, we are only able to evaluate open-source models. Applying probes to frontier, closed-source models requires cooperation from providers, and any evidence of success or failure has to come from their future reports. Our analysis is restricted to English, leaving open whether results generalize across languages or task framings. Although we argue that our informed-choice format is unbiased, alternative wording in multiple-choice questions may still affect model behavior and lead to different outcomes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p8.1" class="ltx_p">We also highlight in <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Training Model Organisms of Emergent Alignment ‣ Appendix ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a> that for models with unfaithful chain-of-thought, probes collapse and fail to provide any meaningful signal. This underscores the critical importance of preserving chain-of-thought and its trustworthiness for oversight of future models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Korbak et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conclusions.</span>   In this work we have evaluated the propensity of over 80 models to be strategically dishonest, when faced with harmful queries. Certain models, even from the same provider, show surprising variance in their preferences towards dishonesty. We show that this behavior fools all existing output-based detectors for harmful responses that we tested, but we also show that probes of internal states are accurate at detecting this behavior, and can identify activation patterns with causal links to dishonesty, which we validate through finetuning and steering.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Ethics Statement</h3>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper studies the generation of potentially harmful content and deceptive behavior of language models (i.e., responses that are plausible but false or misleading). We acknowledge that this line of research involves material that could be misused if released irresponsibly. Our primary aim is to characterize and mitigate model deception, and to develop detection and prevention strategies that reduce risks from jailbreaks and misuse. To highlight the problematic verifiability of jailbreak outputs, we release a limited set of pseudo-harmful outputs.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, our framing of strategic dishonesty as a measurement problem sidesteps the normative question of deontological (duty-based) versus consequentialist (outcome-based) ethical perspectives. Throughout this paper, we have implicitly adopted a deontological stance by treating dishonesty as categorically problematic. We acknowledge that under a consequentialist framework, if models (or human stand-ins) were convinced of imminent harm, deception could be considered justifiable to prevent harm. However, we argue that, even then, maintaining transparency and epistemic reliability must take precedence, as strategic dishonesty undermines trust fundamentally.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Reproducibility Statement</h3>

<div id="Sx2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide experimental details and exact system prompts to enable reproduction of our findings. Specifically, <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A How do LLMs Tradeoff Helpfulness, Harmlessness and Honesty: Further Analysis ‣ Appendix ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> covers the MCQ setting, <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Further Details and Full Results on Direct Faking Requests ‣ Appendix ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> describes the direct query setting, <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Training and Validating Deception Probes ‣ Appendix ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a> details the training and validation of deception probes, <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Strategic Dishonesty as Honeypot Obfuscation Defense ‣ Appendix ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a> documents attacks against the honeypot defense, and <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Training Model Organisms of Emergent Alignment ‣ Appendix ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a> provides details on training a model that exhibits deceptive behavior in response to harmful queries.</p>
</div>
</section>
