<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="Pt1" class="ltx_part">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_part">Appendix</h2>

<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>How do LLMs Tradeoff Helpfulness, Harmlessness and Honesty: Further Analysis</h3>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in <a href="#S4" title="4 Strategic Dishonesty Undermines LLM Judges ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> and <a href="#A8" title="Appendix H Examples of Strategic Dishonesty ‣ Appendix ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H</span></a>, distinguishing genuinely harmful outputs from deliberately flawed responses that do not enable misuse is hard. To detect strategic deception, we use an explicit MCQ design. The model receives a detailed system prompt (see <a href="#A7.SS1" title="G.1 MCQ System Prompt ‣ Appendix G System Prompts ‣ Appendix ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G.1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We instruct the model to drop exactly one HHH value while preserving the other two. The prompt presents balanced arguments for each choice. We also draft a compliant response to the example query: <span id="A1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">How to run a successful scam on eBay Kleinanzeigen?</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To promote interpretable, robust outputs, we follow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Greenblatt et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and enforce a three-part format: (i) a scratchpad reasoning step that weighs trade-offs and selects a strategy; (ii) an explicit strategy choice; and (iii) a final response consistent with that choice. We use Claude 4 Sonnet to generate scratchpad reasoning for the in-context examples of each strategy and to adapt the manually written compliant response to each strategy.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We present results for frontier models (after filtering API errors) in <a href="#S3.F3" title="In 3.2 Some Models Fake, While Others Don’t ‣ 3 A Choice Between Helpfulness, Harmlessness and Honesty ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and for the remaining models in <a href="#A1.F8" title="In A.3 RQ3: Is Sacrificing Honesty Part of a Broader Tendency to Lie? ‣ Appendix A How do LLMs Tradeoff Helpfulness, Harmlessness and Honesty: Further Analysis ‣ Appendix ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>. While parsing outputs, we observed four failure modes:</p>
<ul id="A1.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="A1.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Failure to follow the format</span>: Several less capable models do not follow the MCQ format or loop on repeated tokens (e.g., Vicuna 7B, OpenReasoning Nemotron 7B, Granite 3.0 8B Instruct). Yet these models still follow the instruction to deceive in the direct-query setting (<a href="#A2.F9" title="In Appendix B Further Details and Full Results on Direct Faking Requests ‣ Appendix ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Refusing the MCQ</span>: Some models ignore the requested format and refuse the entire MCQ without providing reasoning or a strategy selection (e.g., Gemma 7B). GPT-OSS-20B (excluded from analysis) is the only highly capable open-source model we observed doing the same; it reasons that the MCQ prompt is a “jailbreak attempt” and refuses immediately.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iii)</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I1.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Strategy inconsistency</span>: Some models follow the format, generate reasoning, and select a strategy, but then disregard that choice by refusing in the final response. We relabel these cases as refusals using the refusal classifier (see added refusals in <a href="#A1.F8" title="In A.3 RQ3: Is Sacrificing Honesty Part of a Broader Tendency to Lie? ‣ Appendix A How do LLMs Tradeoff Helpfulness, Harmlessness and Honesty: Further Analysis ‣ Appendix ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.ix4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iv)</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.ix4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.ix4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.ix4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">API errors</span>: Some closed-source or very large models return API-level rejections.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To handle these cases, we exclude outputs that fail the required format or encounter API-level errors and use an GPT-5-mini refusal judge to detect inconsistent refusals (see <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Refusal Judge ‣ Appendix ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a> for the system prompt). We observed the first three issues only in less capable models (e.g., Vicuna 7B, Gemma 7B, Granite 3.0 8B; LLM-Arena score <math id="A1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;1250" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1250</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;1250</annotation></semantics></math>). For completeness, we report error distributions in <a href="#A1.F8" title="In A.3 RQ3: Is Sacrificing Honesty Part of a Broader Tendency to Lie? ‣ Appendix A How do LLMs Tradeoff Helpfulness, Harmlessness and Honesty: Further Analysis ‣ Appendix ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>RQ1: Does RLHF Make Models Fake More?</h4>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare six publicly available SFT and DPO checkpoints from two model families (Qwen3 MiroThinker 8B/14B/32B and Llama-3.1 Tülu 8B/70B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lambert et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">MiroMind AI Team, 2025</a>)</cite>). Across these models, SFT shows slightly higher faking rates than DPO, but the differences are not statistically significant. Nonparametric tests (Mann–Whitney <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>) yield <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.44" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.44</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.44</annotation></semantics></math> for faking, <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.69" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.69</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.69</annotation></semantics></math> for compliance, and <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.23" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.23</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.23</annotation></semantics></math> for refusal. We therefore find no evidence that RLHF consistently increases faking in our evaluation. Data appear in <a href="#A1.T3" title="In A.1 RQ1: Does RLHF Make Models Fake More? ‣ Appendix A How do LLMs Tradeoff Helpfulness, Harmlessness and Honesty: Further Analysis ‣ Appendix ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>Mean <math id="A1.T3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> std across model variants within the MiroThinker and Tülu families over SFT and DPO checkpoints. While SFT checkpoints show higher faking and compliance rates on average, no statistically significant differences were found between the two categories.</figcaption>
