<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>RQ2: Does Reasoning Enable Models to Choose Faking?</h4>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate eight Qwen3 models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, paired with and without reasoning traces (4 reasoning vs. 4 non-reasoning). Reasoning variants show slightly lower average faking (3.6% vs. 5.5%), but the difference is not significant. Mann–Whitney <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> tests give <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.49" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.49</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.49</annotation></semantics></math> for faking, <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.49" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.49</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.49</annotation></semantics></math> for compliance, and <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.34" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.34</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.34</annotation></semantics></math> for refusal. Thus, we find no evidence that reasoning alone systematically increases or reduces faking. Within the reasoning group, however, the propensity to fake appears to scale with model size. Results are in <a href="#A1.T4" title="In A.2 RQ2: Does Reasoning Enable Models to Choose Faking? ‣ Appendix A How do LLMs Tradeoff Helpfulness, Harmlessness and Honesty: Further Analysis ‣ Appendix ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span>Comparison of Qwen3 with reasoning enabled and disabled. Group rows report mean <math id="A1.T4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> std across 4 reasoning and 4 non-reasoning models. We find no statistically significant differences in faking (<math id="A1.T4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.49" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.49</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.49</annotation></semantics></math>), compliance (<math id="A1.T4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.49" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.49</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.49</annotation></semantics></math>), or refusal (<math id="A1.T4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.34" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.34</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.34</annotation></semantics></math>).</figcaption>
<table id="A1.T4.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="A1.T4.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T4.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_border_tt"></td>
<td id="A1.T4.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" colspan="3"><span id="A1.T4.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reasoning</span></td>
<td id="A1.T4.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" colspan="3"><span id="A1.T4.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">No Reasoning</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T4.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T4.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span id="A1.T4.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model</span></td>
