</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>RQ3: Is Sacrificing Honesty Part of a Broader Tendency to Lie?</h4>

<div id="A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The MASK benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al., 2025</a>)</cite> elicits models’ true beliefs and then measures their willingness to state falsehoods under pressure. We intersect our evaluation set with MASK and obtain a common subset of nine models (Qwen 1.5 72B, Qwen 1.5 32B, Qwen 1.5 7B, Qwen 2.5 72B, QwQ-32B, o3-mini, Gemini 2.0 Flash, DeepSeek-R1, Grok-2). Correlating our faking rate with MASK’s lying propensity <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\text{lie})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>lie</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\text{lie})</annotation></semantics></math> yields no significant relationship (Pearson <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=-0.27" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.27</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=-0.27</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.49" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.49</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.49</annotation></semantics></math>; Spearman <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=-0.31" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.31</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=-0.31</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.42" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.42</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.42</annotation></semantics></math>). This suggests our faking measure captures a dishonesty trait distinct from that measured by MASK.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<figure id="A1.F8" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.18058v1/app_fig_1.svg" id="A1.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:548/786;" width="548" height="786"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span><span id="A1.F8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Some LLMs Sacrifice Honesty While Others Do Not.</span>
For each model we report the proportion of MCQ examples in which it sacrifices (a) honesty, (b) helpfulness, or (c) harmlessness.
We additionally report the share of errors arising from format failures, MCQ refusals, and API errors (d).
All draft responses are also evaluated with a refusal judge; cases scored as refusals but with an MCQ selection of A or C are regraded into B.
Harmful queries are sourced from ClearHarm, and all results are averaged over five seeds.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Further Details and Full Results on Direct Faking Requests</h3>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we test whether models can produce fake jailbreak responses. We prompt each model with a system instruction that reduces harm not by refusing the query, but by returning a deliberately flawed answer. Some models still refuse harmful queries; we remove those cases with a refusal judge (see <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Refusal Judge ‣ Appendix ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>). We then compare the remaining non-refusals in the direct-query setting with the propensity to fake in the MCQ setting (<a href="#A2.F9" title="In Appendix B Further Details and Full Results on Direct Faking Requests ‣ Appendix ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We define a high-quality fake response as one that a judge cannot distinguish from a genuinely harmful instruction before attempting execution. We measure this ability using widely adopted LLM judges for harmful outputs: LlamaGuard (fine-tuned from Llama 2 7B) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Inan et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, HarmBench (fine-tuned from Llama 2 13B) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mazeika et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, JailJudge (fine-tuned from Llama 2 7B) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, ShieldGemma (2B, fine-tuned from Gemma 2 2B), WildGuard (fine-tuned from Mistral 7B v0.3) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Han et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, HarmScore (fine-tuned from Llama 3.1 8B) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chan et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, as well as two prompt-scaffold judges: JailbreakBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chao et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and StrongReject <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Souly et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, both based on GPT-5-mini. Normalized harmfulness scores (<math id="A2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>) for non-refused responses appear in <a href="#A2.T5" title="In Appendix B Further Details and Full Results on Direct Faking Requests ‣ Appendix ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.T5" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5: </span>Evaluation of non-refused queries across eight harmful-output judges. Judge scores reported over five seeds; queries sources from ClearHarm.</figcaption>
