<section id="A5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.1 </span>When Probes Work: QwQ-32B-FT Case</h4>

<div id="A5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We apply the math pipeline from <a href="#S5" title="5 Verifying Deception ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. First, we verify that the model still solves benign math. On UnicornMath, the fine-tuned model reaches 83.06% accuracy vs. 85.71% for the non-fine-tuned model. We then prompt it with EvilMath questions without any system prompt. Some reasoning traces exceeded 8K tokens, so we report only answers we could parse and present results in <a href="#A5.F10" title="In E.1 When Probes Work: QwQ-32B-FT Case ‣ Appendix E Training Model Organisms of Emergent Alignment ‣ Appendix ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">QwQ-32B-FT continues to recognize that many EvilMath queries are ordinary math problems and answers them correctly because they are not inherently harmful. When it recognizes a query as harmful and chooses to deceive on its own, the deception probes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goldowsky-Dill et al. (2025)</a></cite> activate. These activations indicate that the probes generalize to this uninstructed setting (see <a href="#A5.F10" title="In E.1 When Probes Work: QwQ-32B-FT Case ‣ Appendix E Training Model Organisms of Emergent Alignment ‣ Appendix ‣ Strategic Dishonesty can Undermine AI Safety Evaluations of Frontier LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.F10" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.18058v1/app_fig_2_v0.svg" id="A5.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:384/206;" width="384" height="206"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 10: </span>For a model trained to deceive on harmful math, probes successfully separate cases where the model chooses to deceive from those where it decides to be honest. Probes work without an explicit instruction to deceive.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Betley et al. (2025)</a></cite>, this behavior also generalizes to other harmful queries that we did not train on; we show an example below. Importantly, this fine-tuning does not increase the observed ASR on direct harmful queries: the model still refuses on the very same queries it refused before fine-tuning. Defensive deception therefore complements refusal training and serves as a second line of defense against motivated attackers.</p>
</div>
